<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bima people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mbojo, Bimanese</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bima people, who call themselves Mbojo, are an ethnic group of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, speaking the Bima language. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> kingdom and later the Bima Sultanate, which accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the early 17th century under the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-gowa">Gowa Sultanate</a> of Makassar. Most are Muslims, and many live by farming and trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bima-people.jpg" alt="Bima people" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BayuAjisaka · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bumi_Bata_Mbani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
